## Week 1 — Analysing the Existing System & Identifying Limitations

- Studied the existing minor project in detail: a topological-geometric trading system that models the stock market as a graph and uses a Temporal Convolutional Network (TCN) to predict next-day directional price movement for Indian equity stocks.
- Analysed the current feature engineering pipeline: understood how the system builds a correlation-based adjacency matrix from stock return data and computes a Laplacian residual signal to capture a stock's deviation from its network neighbours.
- Reviewed the existing topological feature extraction using Persistent Homology (Betti numbers H0, H1), which attempts to capture the "shape" of the market at each time step.
- Identified critical limitations in the graph construction module: the correlation threshold (τ = 0.5) is hardcoded, making the adjacency matrix insensitive to varying market regimes — in high-correlation crash periods the graph becomes fully connected (signal collapses), and in low-correlation periods it becomes empty (signal = 0).
- Identified that the model has no explicit mechanism to distinguish between market regimes (bull, bear, high-volatility, sideways), meaning the same model weights are applied regardless of the current economic environment.
- Identified a scale mismatch across input feature channels: RSI ranges 0–100 while log returns range −0.05 to +0.05, creating gradient instability during training.
- Observed that the training pipeline used only 10 epochs with a flat learning rate, resulting in an undertrained model (loss was still declining at termination).
- Surveyed literature on Graph Attention Networks (GAT), Persistent Homology, Walsh-Hadamard spectral analysis, and regime-aware machine learning to identify candidate improvements.
- Shortlisted four improvement directions and discussed feasibility with project guide: (1) adaptive graph construction with attention, (2) enhanced topological features, (3) explicit regime labelling, (4) training pipeline upgrades.

