## Week 2 — Research & Design of Improved Feature Architecture

- Conducted a detailed study of Graph Attention Networks (GAT): understood the attention mechanism `a(i,j) = softmax(LeakyReLU(a^T [Wh_i || Wh_j]))` which assigns learned importance weights to edges rather than binary thresholding.
- Researched Persistent Homology and Vietoris-Rips filtration: understood how H0 persistence (connected components) and H1 persistence (loops/cycles) in the correlation distance matrix encode structural fragmentation vs cohesion of the market graph.
- Studied Walsh-Hadamard Transform as a spectral tool for binary pattern decomposition across stock return sequences — identified its utility for capturing market-wide synchronisation in a complementary way to GAT.
- Designed the expanded 16-channel feature set:
  - C1–C7: Standard technical indicators (Log Return, Volatility, RSI, MACD, ATR, Bollinger %B, Z-Score)
  - C8: GAT-derived graph attention signal (replaces old Laplacian residual)
  - C9: Walsh spectral score (market synchronisation measure)
  - C10: H0 Persistence entropy (market fragmentation)
  - C11: H1 Persistence entropy (topological loops/cycles)
  - C12: Beta stability (rate of structural topological change)
  - C13–C16: One-hot regime labels (Crash / High-Volatility / Bull / Sideways)
- Finalised the walk-forward validation framework for the Indian market: defined 8 rolling windows covering key economic regimes — Demonetization (2016), GST launch (2017), IL&FS crisis (2018), General Elections (2019), COVID crash (2020).
- Reviewed literature on selective normalisation: concluded that topological features (GAT, H0, H1) must NOT be Z-scored because their stress-event spikes encode absolute magnitude information that normalisation would destroy.

