## Week 4 — Training Pipeline Upgrades & Walk-Forward Execution

- Upgraded the model training function with three key improvements:
  - Cosine LR Annealing: learning rate decays smoothly from 1×10⁻³ to 1×10⁻⁵ over 30 epochs, replacing the flat learning rate that caused the optimiser to oscillate around the loss minimum.
  - Gradient Clipping: applied max-norm clipping (norm = 1.0) to prevent exploding gradients arising from the heterogeneous channel magnitudes in the 16-channel input tensor.
  - Early Stopping: patience of 5 epochs with minimum delta 1×10⁻⁵ to prevent overfitting on smaller training windows while allowing adequate convergence on larger ones.
- Implemented selective Z-score normalisation: applied only to C1–C7 (standard price and technical indicator channels) to resolve scale mismatch; C8–C16 left in natural units to preserve the absolute magnitude of topological stress-event spikes.
- Fixed a data leakage vulnerability in the evaluation pipeline: normalisation statistics (mean, std) are now computed strictly on the training split and passed as fixed parameters to the test split, eliminating look-ahead bias.
- Executed the full walk-forward training and evaluation across five windows (2016–2020): model trained per window on all available prior data, tested on the immediately following unseen year.
- Implemented and ran a 10-baseline comparison suite on identical walk-forward windows: Always-Up, Random, Momentum (1-day and 5-day), RSI threshold, MACD crossover, Bollinger %B, Logistic Regression (16-channel), Random Forest (16-channel), and a TCN trained on standard 7 technical indicators only.
- Identified a methodological inconsistency in the baseline comparison: the 7-channel TCN baseline used only 10 epochs and a flat learning rate, making the comparison unfair. Upgraded the baseline training pipeline to match (30 epochs, cosine LR, gradient clipping) and re-ran for a rigorous like-for-like evaluation.

